1
00:00:00,018 --> 00:00:05,160
How can your smartphone pinpoint your
exact location anywhere on Earth just by

2
00:00:05,160 --> 00:00:06,880
receiving silent radio signals?

3
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:12,880
In reality, the Global Positioning System
is a completely one-way passive broadcast

4
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:13,680
network.

5
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:18,580
GPS operates independently of
telephone or Internet reception.

6
00:00:19,380 --> 00:00:21,300
Owned by the United States Space Force,

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:24,980
the satellite system is
freely accessible to anyone.

8
00:00:25,683 --> 00:00:31,140
The constellation consists of at least
24 active satellites arranged across six

9
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:32,460
distinct orbital planes.

10
00:00:33,246 --> 00:00:38,460
The first prototype spacecraft
went into orbit in 1978 before

11
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:41,380
reaching full operational status in 1995.

12
00:00:43,026 --> 00:00:47,680
Each satellite completes two full orbits
around Earth during every sidereal day.

13
00:00:48,372 --> 00:00:53,580
As they orbit, these satellites continuously
broadcast radio signals containing

14
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:56,140
their exact position and precise time.

15
00:00:56,820 --> 00:00:58,820
Your phone acts as a silent receiver,

16
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:03,100
quietly catching these incoming
radio transmissions from the sky.

17
00:01:04,824 --> 00:01:06,480
To figure out your exact location,

18
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,080
your device converts time measurements
directly into distance measurements.

19
00:01:11,930 --> 00:01:17,420
GPS satellites circle the Earth in medium
orbit at an altitude of approximately

20
00:01:17,420 --> 00:01:19,942
20,200 kilometres.

21
00:01:20,397 --> 00:01:25,500
Radio signals broadcast from these satellites
travel through space at the constant

22
00:01:25,500 --> 00:01:26,300
speed of light.

23
00:01:26,845 --> 00:01:31,920
Crucially, the speed of radio waves is
constant and completely independent of the

24
00:01:31,920 --> 00:01:32,720
satellite's speed.

25
00:01:33,525 --> 00:01:38,920
Each satellite continually broadcasts a
pseudorandom sequence of ones and zeros

26
00:01:38,920 --> 00:01:40,080
known to the receiver.

27
00:01:40,948 --> 00:01:46,220
The transmission message includes a
timestamp recording the exact time the signal

28
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:47,020
left the satellite.

29
00:01:47,824 --> 00:01:49,520
When your device receives the signal,

30
00:01:50,100 --> 00:01:53,760
it records the arrival time
using its own internal clock.

31
00:01:54,526 --> 00:01:57,560
By subtracting the transmission
time from the arrival time,

32
00:01:58,040 --> 00:02:00,300
the receiver calculates the signal delay.

33
00:02:00,978 --> 00:02:06,080
Multiplying this time value by the speed
of light yields a pseudorange rather than

34
00:02:06,080 --> 00:02:07,040
an exact distance.

35
00:02:07,781 --> 00:02:13,000
Because light moves so fast, a tiny
timing offset of just 10 nanoseconds

36
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,040
causes a 3 metre distance error.

37
00:02:16,726 --> 00:02:20,860
A single distance measurement places your
device somewhere on a giant spherical

38
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:22,980
shell centred on that satellite.

39
00:02:23,742 --> 00:02:28,340
To narrow down your location, your phone
needs signals from additional satellites

40
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:29,300
orbiting above.

41
00:02:30,044 --> 00:02:32,780
When you receive a distance
measurement from a second satellite,

42
00:02:33,240 --> 00:02:34,680
a second sphere is created.

43
00:02:35,740 --> 00:02:40,060
Taking several distance measurements allows
a receiver to find the point where the

44
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:41,140
spherical shells meet.

45
00:02:41,880 --> 00:02:46,660
Your receiver generates a position fix by
finding where multiple spherical shells

46
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:47,460
meet.

47
00:02:47,683 --> 00:02:52,380
Adding a third satellite creates a third
spherical surface around that third

48
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:53,480
broadcasting source.

49
00:02:54,136 --> 00:02:58,860
Combining multiple distance measurements
allows the receiver to determine the point

50
00:02:58,860 --> 00:02:59,820
where the shells meet.

51
00:03:00,508 --> 00:03:03,300
In special cases where
elevation is already known,

52
00:03:03,860 --> 00:03:05,980
three satellites can determine a position.

53
00:03:06,750 --> 00:03:12,180
This geometric technique of calculating
positions using measured distances is

54
00:03:12,180 --> 00:03:13,989
known as trilateration.

55
00:03:15,448 --> 00:03:20,140
Trilateration with three spheres assumes
that your receiver clock is perfectly

56
00:03:20,140 --> 00:03:21,780
synchronized with the satellites.

57
00:03:22,577 --> 00:03:28,020
GPS satellites carry extremely stable
atomic clocks that are monitored and

58
00:03:28,020 --> 00:03:30,220
synchronized regularly by ground stations.

59
00:03:31,190 --> 00:03:32,860
Because your phone clock is inaccurate,

60
00:03:33,460 --> 00:03:38,017
measured travel times produce biased
distances called pseudoranges.

61
00:03:38,455 --> 00:03:42,180
To solve this problem without needing
an impractical receiver clock,

62
00:03:42,740 --> 00:03:45,120
the system uses signals
from a fourth satellite.

63
00:03:45,976 --> 00:03:51,360
Your GPS receiver requires signals from
at least four satellites to solve its

64
00:03:51,360 --> 00:03:52,380
navigation equations.

65
00:03:53,261 --> 00:03:57,680
With four satellite signals, your phone
calculates four unknown mathematical

66
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:59,100
quantities simultaneously.

67
00:04:00,567 --> 00:04:05,580
These unknowns are three spatial coordinates
representing position and the exact

68
00:04:05,580 --> 00:04:07,280
deviation of your phone clock.

69
00:04:08,007 --> 00:04:12,420
Solving the navigation equations gives
receiver position and clock deviation,

70
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:15,880
providing cheap and
highly accurate timing.

71
00:04:16,640 --> 00:04:20,660
This clever mathematical approach
keeps consumer receivers light,

72
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:23,620
affordable, and practical for daily use.

73
00:04:25,315 --> 00:04:27,040
Even with four satellite signals,

74
00:04:27,660 --> 00:04:32,600
GPS would fail quickly without accounting
for Albert Einstein's theories of

75
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:33,400
relativity.

76
00:04:33,839 --> 00:04:39,300
Special relativity predicts that time
passes slower for objects moving at higher

77
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:41,220
speeds relative to an observer.

78
00:04:41,987 --> 00:04:47,100
Because satellites travel at
roughly 3,874 metres per second,

79
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:49,200
their atomic clocks tick slower.

80
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:55,260
This speed effect causes orbital clocks
to lose about 7.2 microseconds per

81
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:56,820
day compared to ground clocks.

82
00:04:57,635 --> 00:05:02,740
Meanwhile, general relativity shows that
gravity slows down time closer to massive

83
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:03,880
bodies like Earth.

84
00:05:04,563 --> 00:05:07,340
Because satellites operate
high above Earth's surface,

85
00:05:08,020 --> 00:05:11,300
they experience weaker gravity
than receivers on the ground.

86
00:05:12,072 --> 00:05:16,040
This weaker gravity causes satellite
clocks to tick faster by about

87
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:18,780
45.8 microseconds per day.

88
00:05:19,443 --> 00:05:21,260
Combining both relativistic effects,

89
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:27,320
satellite clocks run faster by
approximately 38.6 microseconds every

90
00:05:27,320 --> 00:05:28,120
day.

91
00:05:28,310 --> 00:05:33,212
If uncorrected, this timing difference
would cause location errors of roughly 11.4

92
00:05:33,212 --> 00:05:35,120
kilometres per day.

93
00:05:35,783 --> 00:05:41,120
To correct this, engineers pre-adjust the
atomic clock frequencies before launching

94
00:05:41,120 --> 00:05:42,400
the satellites into space.

95
00:05:43,214 --> 00:05:47,220
They slow the clock rate
from 10.23 megahertz down to

96
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:52,160
10.22999999543 megahertz.

97
00:05:52,760 --> 00:05:57,660
This pre-launch adjustment allows orbital
clocks to stay synchronized with time on

98
00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:58,460
Earth's surface.

99
00:06:00,099 --> 00:06:02,560
Once your device processes
the satellite signals,

100
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:06,340
it calculates raw three-dimensional
position coordinates.

101
00:06:07,247 --> 00:06:10,720
The receiver then converts these
spatial numbers into standard latitude,

102
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:12,900
longitude, and height.

103
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:18,680
Consumer devices like smartphones can
achieve horizontal positioning accuracy of

104
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:20,940
4.9 metres or better.

105
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:24,840
When paired with assistive
services like Wi-Fi positioning,

106
00:06:25,660 --> 00:06:28,260
positioning performance
becomes even more reliable.

107
00:06:29,174 --> 00:06:34,160
High-end applications like land surveying
can even achieve sub-millimetre accuracy

108
00:06:34,680 --> 00:06:35,940
with long-term measurements.

109
00:06:36,892 --> 00:06:42,000
These calculated coordinates may be
displayed on a moving map display or used by

110
00:06:42,000 --> 00:06:42,800
another system.

111
00:06:43,435 --> 00:06:47,940
An internal tracker algorithm combines
successive measurements to predict movement

112
00:06:47,940 --> 00:06:50,120
and filter out random errors.

113
00:06:50,952 --> 00:06:53,660
By analyzing signal
Doppler frequency shifts,

114
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:58,020
your device can also calculate
your exact velocity and direction.

115
00:06:58,868 --> 00:07:03,540
More advanced navigation systems combine
these satellite data with internal

116
00:07:03,540 --> 00:07:05,680
compasses or motion sensors.

117
00:07:06,466 --> 00:07:11,140
Your phone finds your exact location
by listening to broadcast timestamps,

118
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:14,960
measuring radio travel times,
and calculating geometry.

119
00:07:16,044 --> 00:07:18,480
Next time you follow that
blue dot on your screen,

120
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:23,460
consider how orbital geometry and
atomic time keeping guide your path.
